# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  API SERVICE, the ONLY project holding privileged credentials.
#  Never copy any value below into a frontend project.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Placeholders only. Nothing in this file is a real credential, and nothing in
# it should ever become one, `npm run guard` fails the build if a key, token or
# password-bearing URL appears in a tracked file.
#
# REQUIRED means lib/env.ts refuses to boot without it.
# OPTIONAL means the feature it configures turns itself off when absent.

# ── Supabase ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── REQUIRED ──
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# PRIVILEGED. Bypasses RLS entirely. Used ONLY for the enumerated
# operations in lib/db/rls.ts, each audited (doc 04 section 6.3).
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# JWKS issuer for access-token verification. Must end with /auth/v1, pointing
# it at the project URL makes login succeed and every authenticated request 401.
SUPABASE_JWT_ISSUER=https://your-project.supabase.co/auth/v1

# ── Postgres ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── REQUIRED ──
# RUNTIME: Supavisor TRANSACTION pooler on :6543. Connecting to :5432 from
# serverless exhausts max_connections and takes down all five apps at once
# (doc 08 section 2.4). env.ts refuses a non-pooler URL.
#
# PERCENT-ENCODE the password. Next.js runs dotenv-expand over .env files, so a
# literal $ makes it substitute $xxx with an empty variable and hand the driver a
# silently truncated credential -- which fails as "password authentication failed
# for user postgres", pointing at the wrong thing entirely. Write $ as %24,
# @ as %40, : as %3A, / as %2F. Quoting the value does NOT stop the expansion.
#
# Left EMPTY on purpose rather than filled with a realistic-looking example:
# `npm run guard` refuses a password-bearing Postgres URL in any tracked file,
# and it cannot tell a placeholder from the real thing, which is the correct
# behaviour for a check whose job is to notice a leaked credential.
#
#   Format: postgresql://USER:PW@HOST:6543/postgres
DATABASE_URL=

# MIGRATIONS AND SCRIPTS ONLY, never used by a running function. Port 5432.
DIRECT_URL=

# ── Upstash: rate limiting, cache, durable queue ────────────────── REQUIRED ──
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=https://your-db.upstash.io
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=
QSTASH_TOKEN=
QSTASH_CURRENT_SIGNING_KEY=
QSTASH_NEXT_SIGNING_KEY=

# ── Job authentication ─────────────────────────────────────────── REQUIRED ──
# Vercel Cron sends this as a bearer token. Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
CRON_SECRET=

# ── ISR purge on CMS publish ───────────────────────────────────── REQUIRED ──
REVALIDATE_SECRET=
LANDING_URL=http://localhost:3004
API_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ── CORS: explicit allowlist. Never "*", never a reflected Origin. REQUIRED ──
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3001,http://localhost:3002,http://localhost:3003,http://localhost:3004

# ── Application URLs used inside outgoing email ────────────────── REQUIRED ──
# Every staff invitation, invoice notice and reschedule decision builds its link
# from one of these. They default to an empty string, which produces an email
# containing a link to "/login" with no host in front of it, a dead link in the
# one message a new colleague needs in order to sign in at all.
PORTAL_URL=http://localhost:3001
INTERNAL_URL=http://localhost:3002

# ── Seed (npm run db:seed) ─────────────────────────────────────── OPTIONAL ──
# The first Head account. Roles and grants seed unconditionally; the account is
# only created when SEED_HEAD_EMAIL is set. Leave SEED_HEAD_PASSWORD unset and
# the script generates one, prints it once, and never stores it. An existing
# account is never modified, so re-running can't reset anyone's password.
SEED_HEAD_EMAIL=
SEED_HEAD_NAME=
SEED_HEAD_PASSWORD=

# ── Email: the ONLY outbound channel (doc 08 section 4) ───────── REQUIRED* ──
#
# *REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION. Unset in development, sends are rendered and logged
# instead, so the whole pipeline can be exercised without a key.
#
# DELIVERABILITY IS PART OF SHIPPING THIS. There is no second channel to fall
# back on, so the sending domain needs SPF, DKIM and DMARC before real traffic.
# EMAIL_FROM must be on a domain verified in Resend; an unverified one fails with
# a 4xx that this code correctly treats as permanent and will never retry.
#
# The production domain is NOT yet decided, see docs/14 section 3.8B. Do not
# copy a domain out of this file; put the one the business actually owns here.
RESEND_API_KEY=
EMAIL_FROM=Metroscope <noreply@your-domain.example>
EMAIL_REPLY_TO=halo@your-domain.example

# ── Google Calendar + Meet (doc 03 section 6.2, doc 07 section 6) ─ OPTIONAL ──
#
# Server-side sync of lessons onto ONE SHARED BUSINESS CALENDAR, using a service
# account. Absent, scheduling works exactly as before and every lesson still
# offers "Add to Google Calendar", the integration turns itself off rather than
# taking the API down (lib/google/credentials.ts).
#
# ⚠️ ALL THREE OR NONE. Two of three throws at startup, because a half-configured
# calendar syncs nothing and reports nothing.
#
# ⚠️ SERVER-ONLY. None of these may ever be prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_, put in a
# frontend project, returned by an endpoint, or written to the database. The
# private key is the whole account.
#
# Google Cloud setup, exactly:
#   1. Create a project and ENABLE THE GOOGLE CALENDAR API. Nothing else. Meet
#      conferences are created through Calendar, so the Meet API is not needed.
#   2. Create a service account. No IAM roles are required: it needs no access
#      to the Google Cloud project itself.
#   3. Create a JSON key for it. Copy `client_email` and `private_key` below;
#      DO NOT COMMIT THE JSON FILE, and delete your download afterwards.
#   4. In Google Calendar (as a human), create a calendar for lessons, then
#      Settings → Share with specific people → add the service account's email
#      with "Make changes to events".
#   5. From that calendar's settings, copy "Calendar ID" (it looks like an
#      address ending in @group.calendar.google.com) into GOOGLE_CALENDAR_ID.
#
# The private key is a PEM with newlines. Keep it on one line with literal \n
# escapes, wrapped in double quotes, the code un-escapes it.
GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL=
GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_PRIVATE_KEY="-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\n...\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\n"
# NOT "primary": a service account's own primary calendar is invisible to
# humans, so events created there are lost. Use the shared calendar's ID.
GOOGLE_CALENDAR_ID=

# ── Monitoring ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── OPTIONAL ──
# Absent means the Sentry SDK never initialises, so local dev and CI need no
# account. A DSN is a write-only ingest key, not a credential, docs/08 §6a.
SENTRY_DSN=

# ── Test harness ───────────────────────────────────────────────── OPTIONAL ──
# Where the suites point. Unset, they assume the local dev servers above.
# API_TEST_URL=http://localhost:3000/api/v1
# SITE_TEST_URL=http://localhost:3004
